WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
I like rugby but I'm not a good player at it;&nbsp;
there are some interesting consequences of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:08.700
rules I want to explore, and this is actually&nbsp;
a famous piece of applied maths about a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.540
world sporting problem. And I'll explain some&nbsp;
of the rules here just for clarity; uh you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:19.200
score points by putting the ball down over a line&nbsp;-
it's called a try - very like in American football&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.420
a touchdown. Point about this though is that once&nbsp;
you've scored a try in rugby you then also get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.960
chance to get some bonus points with what they&nbsp;
call a conversion where you kick over the posts.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.880
So full disclosure, in American football you put&nbsp;
the ball down over the- as a touchdown, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.300
then get to take the kick from in front of&nbsp;
the post. Is that right? I think that's right?&nbsp;

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:45.120
Never played American football but I'm&nbsp;
reliably informed. In rugby you cannot do that;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.400
you have to take the conversion kick somewhere in&nbsp;
line with where you put the ball down. So if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.200
put the ball down right at the edge of the pitch,&nbsp;
like close to the the touch line - which is quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.160
common because people are sort of bleeding you out&nbsp;
there as you get tackled - you have to take a kick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.540
really close to the touchline, but you can choose&nbsp;
how far back to take it. That is our question: if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.440
you have the choice, which you do every time you&nbsp;
score a try, how far back should you take the kick?&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.480
If you are really close if you like then you can&nbsp;
imagine the post being at a weird angle; but if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.720
go really far back - I mean technically I think you&nbsp;
can go to the other end of the pitch but I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:23.220
think it would be clever for a range of reasons. Even&nbsp;
if you are a good kicker, unlike me.
- No we can't!

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:28.740
(Brady: It's in!)

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.640
(First time)
- The real issue is that maybe your&nbsp;
advice would change. So let's get this one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.780
thing out of the way; if you put the try down&nbsp;
under the posts you have to take the kick in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.540
line with that. And there isn't really a problem&nbsp;
here, you just go back far enough that you can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.100
it over the bar; and there's no worry because the&nbsp;
further back you go the narrower the angle looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.240
because you're further away. Obviously if you&nbsp;
try and take it from the try line itself you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.480
going to struggle to clear the thing. But it's the&nbsp;
first modeling thing I want to get out of the way,&nbsp;

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.860
is that let's just assume that under the post&nbsp;
is not a problem, you take it close enough and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.700
you're fine. If you put a try down to the side&nbsp;
of the posts - and this is where I want to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.080
you on the diagram. So here's a picture of rugby&nbsp;
pitch, I've got a nice 3D model, uh I think you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.320
might recognise the software, this is GeoGebra.
So this point here is where it's called the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.400
try, I need to take a kick somewhere&nbsp;
in line with that. And if I move this point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.900
the line moves up. We just talked about&nbsp;
going under the posts, no big deal. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.520
if you're to the side of the post you&nbsp;
can imagine taking the kick from over here,

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:33.300
looks a bit difficult if I sort of zoom the view&nbsp;
around - like it's a terrible angle. But if you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:39.720
the kick from really really far away it's arguable&nbsp;
that maybe you've got a better angle but the range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.440
starts to become an issue. And if you go really&nbsp;
far away, like way down the other end of the pitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.780
the goal is just a really long way away. And&nbsp;
even if the range isn't a problem the angle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.340
is narrowing because you're getting further away&nbsp;
from it. So the question I have here is how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.340 --> 00:03:00.120
best get the angle of the posts to be maximized,&nbsp;
because that's going to be what matters for your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.300
accuracy of the kick. There are some other things,
like can you clear the crossbar? We've talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.500
that a bit - if you're too close it's going to be&nbsp;
tricky. Incidentally there is- there's a five metre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.620
line on the rugby pitches here. And I think a good&nbsp;
rule of thumb is that at the angle most people kick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.640
at, about 30 degrees they say, as long as you're&nbsp;
a distance the other side of that line you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.000
likely to be able to clear the crossbar so we&nbsp;
can just dodge that part of the model. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.300
(You're saying if you're closer than that it's almost&nbsp;too steep?)
- Yeah you're going to have to sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.380
do a proper chip and actually that's really hard&nbsp;
to give any power anyway, and it's just unnatural.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.120
I looked up apparently 30 degrees is roughly&nbsp;
what a good kicker will kick at, and at about 26&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.340
metres per second goes a long way-
- (You mean 30&nbsp;degrees up, not 30 degrees angle?)
- 30 degrees elevation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.720
yeah. Everyone thinks that 45 degrees is the&nbsp;
best but it turns out if you as a kicker, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.860
kick and aim to do 45 degrees you lose a lot of&nbsp;
power because it's not the way your your leg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.300
moves. So this is the real model, look at the&nbsp;
rugby players and they kick it about 30 degrees.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.200
And that also is going to solve a problem&nbsp;
with range, is that if they do that and they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.900
professionals they can kick a long way, like maybe&nbsp;
beyond 30 metres. Which means even if you went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.160
the halfway line there's still a chance that you&nbsp;
could get it long enough if you could- I'm aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.720
that I should probably go and try this but uh I&nbsp;
don't think I could do that. But that means the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:14.820
modeling is really how do you get the best angle?&nbsp;
And the extremes are - I know you like thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.940
about extremes - if you're really close to the try&nbsp;
line the angle is going to be really narrow, we saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.900
on there, and if you're really far away it's&nbsp;
also going to be narrow. Somewhere in the middle is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:27.420
an optimum, and that sounds like a task for maths.
So that's our job. The first thing to do is to draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:34.800
some diagrams, so I'm going to show on here a angle.&nbsp;
Here is the angle, they call it the angle subtended;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.160
um most people have never heard that word unless&nbsp;
they remember some circle theorem from school, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.480
it's the angle that the posts make to your point&nbsp;
here. And you see that actually the measurement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.800
here, if I'm really close to the try location,
the angle's down to three degrees. That's a super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.640
narrow angle, and if you're really far away&nbsp;
it goes down to single digits as well but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.980
somewhere in the middle I'm into double digits,&nbsp;
10.11 degrees angle - that's looking like a best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:01.080
place. But where is the best place? And does it&nbsp;
change when you move your try location around?  Yes.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.820
And I don't think it's obvious. So I- if you&nbsp;
interview rugby players about where they do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:12.300
I am sure that they are solving this problem every&nbsp;
day without doing a hefty amount of maths. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:18.000
you got down to it, and this is now big business&nbsp;
in sport, getting those margins optimised; even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.480
if there's a place that you should take it from&nbsp;
and then you adjust for your range and the wind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.980
distance and how tired you're feeling it would&nbsp;
be good knowledge and I think we should solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:31.140
it. Let me draw a rugby pitch; those are- this is my&nbsp;
idealised goal posts and let's say we're working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:36.360
here and you score try there, this is the line&nbsp;
that you are allowed to take a kick from. And just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.420
to clarify, let's say you take the kick from here&nbsp;
then this angle is what we're trying to maximise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:49.800
Call it theta, let's call it theta. Really close&nbsp;
in, the angle is going to be tiny; really far away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.580
the angle gets smaller as well - there must be&nbsp;
some optimal thing, how do we find it? Now you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:58.440
could do this the long way. I recommend it as&nbsp;
an exercise for any interested parties, you'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.940
need some calculus because we're optimising, we're&nbsp;
maximising something, and it gets super messy so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.300
don't do it unless you're brave. There's a better&nbsp;
way - and this is quite common in mathematics, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.420
that sometimes you just need to draw a better&nbsp;
picture. There's a few things you should know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:15.480
this is the distance between the posts, actually&nbsp;
I think it's 5.6 metres. If you change the rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.920
of rugby though we can still work with d. And the&nbsp;
distance we score the try at I'm going to call x&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:24.240
and that's to the middle of the post. So it feels&nbsp;
like a good place to measure where you score the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.100
try. So that's our variable x, and what we're&nbsp;
trying to decide is this distance here, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:34.560
call it y.
- (How far should you pace back with the&nbsp;ball and take your kick.)
- Yeah so mathematically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.240
we're trying to find a function uh of x - so y&nbsp;
equals something to do with x - practically you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.360
said exactly the same thing, which is how far
should you go back from where you put it down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:47.100
Any three points you put on paper, or anywhere&nbsp;
else, you can draw a circle through, a unique circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:51.720
through three points. And there are three points&nbsp;
we care about here, which is quite important: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:56.340
left post, the right post, and your kick position&nbsp;
that you may or may not choose. Wherever you choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:59.340
there is a circle that goes through those three.&nbsp;
There's a thing that people learn in schools about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.940
circles, with a whole bunch of circle theorems.&nbsp;
Circles have beautiful properties and one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:08.460
those properties is in play right here because&nbsp;
this circle crosses the blue dotted line there,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:14.340
that's why I went through that, but it also crosses&nbsp;
here. And if I draw the other angle from there

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.360
we've got in the UK, a GCSE level circle&nbsp;
theorem going on here, we've got a segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.280
um or rather a chord across a circle, the segments&nbsp;
the other side of it, subtending an angle on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:32.700
edge of the circle. And the theorem says that angles&nbsp;
subtended off the same chord are the same. Now this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.540
is nothing to do with where you've kicked from,&nbsp;
but it's showing us that if you chose to kick from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:41.760
there you would get the same result. Which means&nbsp;
whatever distance you choose there is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.240
place where you get the same angle. Maybe this&nbsp;
is not a surprise; close in it's going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:50.460
narrow, and really far away is going to be narrow.&nbsp;
So you're seeing those pairings and the circle is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:54.600
backing us up. But here's another observation: so&nbsp;
